A||N||6||Elenor84||Starter Alignment(won't fit back in)||||1||
Z||000000||Elenor84||09-03-2001||09:21 PM||||Hey guys...I took my starter out to fix it and now  it won't fit back in.  It goes in fine but as I tighten the bolts it moves out of alignment.  I never had this problem before.  What is it?  I was thinking if I used washers on the bolts it will stay in alignment.  Would this work?  One more thing.  I don't have a spacer between my starter and the mount.  When I did have it in the bolts came loose.<BR>Thx guys!<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||09-04-2001||08:03 AM||||If you had a spacer before you'll need the spacer.<P>The spacer had nothing to do with the bolts coming loose.  I lost a starter bolt one time and mine doesn't have a starter shim/spacer.  <P>Use "blue" thread locker on the bolt threads and you should have no problems with the bolts coming loose.<P>I wouldn't recommend using washers instead of the correct spacer/shim.  If you've lost the starter shim, you should be able to find one at any parts store.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-04-2001||10:15 AM||||When you say the starter "maligns" itself as you tighten the bolts, I immediately envision gnashing of teeth-!  Is it possible you just need to rotate the pinion (or rotate the engine, if that's easier) slightly?  Just a thought-maybe that's too easy...<P>----------------------------<P>Of course you're right, Songman (below)... what was I thinking??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">    <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||Songman||09-04-2001||12:48 PM||||The teeth shouldn't be out on the starter until the solenoid engages so there should be no rubbing there. <P>Put the shim back in and let it line itself up. If you have the bolts in the holes and everything it will pull itself to where it needs to be. <P>I just did my starter on my Indy recently and know it is a pain. You may just have some sort of optical illusion going on. It may just look crooked...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||09-04-2001||02:10 PM||||Look on the end of the shaft housing for interference marks.  The teeth won't hit the ringear/flywheel as Songman said but the housing might w/o the spacer being used. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||09-05-2001||07:40 AM||||The spacer is there to set the center to center distance between the starter gear and the ring gear of the flywheel/flexplate.  If there was one when you took the starter off then you need to put it back.  Without it you will be trying to jam the two gears together when you hit the starter switch.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Elenor84||09-05-2001||05:30 PM||||There was no shim on there when I took it off.  I put it on myself the first time.<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
